  "answer": "Yes, sometimes—but correction is not an unrestricted opportunity to broaden the specification.\n\n**Later correction — Manual practice:** The Manual describes section 65A as permitting correction of clerical errors or obvious mistakes in published applications where fair and reasonable, including where section 65 would not allow the amendment. If the Registrar is satisfied that the amendment request would be granted, the request must be published on Australian Trade Mark Search so others can oppose it. [TMM/Part9/4/4/7] The Manual also states that section 65 does not provide for adding items or classes where the original items were clearly specified or qualified and correctly classified. [TMM/Part14/4/4/2/6]\n\n**Further detail — Manual practice:** Yes. Adequate information should accompany a specification where the goods or services cannot be described using the preferred terms. [TMM/Part10/5/5/1/3] If the specification is too vague or unclear to classify, the examiner should request further information, directly relevant to the application, from the applicant’s website or published trade brochures or pamphlets. [TMM/Part14/4/4/6/2]\n\n**Regulations:** The supplied regulation 4.2(1)(c) requires the application to specify its goods and/or services. [TMR1995/r4.2(1)(c)] Sections 65 and 65A and regulation 4.4(7) themselves are not supplied, so the explanation above of their operation rests on the Manual’s account, not direct verification of those legislative provisions."
